  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Introduction to Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nIn this lesson, students build and test a paper prototype of a web app, then compare their designs with classmates. What they discover is the fun part: without any instructions, most groups make surprisingly similar choices about where to put things. That's the lesson's central insight — layout patterns aren't random, they're intentional design decisions that designers make to guide how users think and move. Students leave with names for the structural components they've been using all along and an early sense that they've already been thinking like designers.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Paper App Prototype Activity Guides (1 per student)\n- Print and cut mystery component sets (1 per pair)\n- Print copies of the Unit Guides (1 per student)\n- Provide tape or glue (1 per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nHave you ever opened a brand new app and immediately knew where to look for the menu? Or tapped exactly where you expected a button to be? Today you're going to find out why by building your own app out of paper. You'll design a layout, test it with a classmate, and probably discover something surprising along the way: the choices you make on your own look a lot like the apps you already love. That's not a coincidence. That's design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "name": "Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore students generate anything, they make predictions: given a prompt for a gourmet pizza restaurant website, what sections will probably appear? Then they see the AI's output and compare it to what they guessed. That prediction moment matters — it gets students thinking about AI as a pattern-matching system rather than a magic box, which is exactly the framing the lesson then formalizes. Students learn how AI uses training data to recognize patterns and generate predictions, get introduced to prompt engineering, and see for the first time that their HTML and CSS live in two separate files. The gap between a vague prompt and a specific one is made concrete through side-by-side comparisons before students try it themselves.",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore you build anything today, you're going to make a prediction. If you gave AI this prompt — \"Build a modern landing page for a gourmet pizza restaurant with a red and charcoal color palette\" — what do you think would show up? Make your guess, then find out if you were right. What you discover will tell you a lot about how AI actually works: not magic, not random, but pattern recognition. That's the foundation everything else in this unit is built on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
      "name": "Intro to CSS",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do we make our web app reflect our design choices?**\n\nThe warm-up does something visually striking: students sketch what a styled webpage would look like without any CSS, then annotate what stays the same and what disappears. It makes the separation between HTML and CSS immediately concrete — structure stays, style vanishes. From there, students are introduced to the Front-End Developer role and the prompt.md file: a structured planning tool with three questions (goals, constraints, edge cases) that they'll use to write better prompts before generating their stylesheet. Side-by-side prompt comparisons make the quality difference explicit before students try it themselves. The lesson ends with students applying CSS to their own web app and reflecting on what the AI changed and why.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we make our web app reflect our design choices?**\n\nWhat would your favorite website look like if all the styling suddenly disappeared? Same structure, same content — but no colors, no fonts, no spacing. Just raw HTML. Today you'll actually sketch that out, and then you'll learn how to bring the style back — by writing better prompts. You'll meet the prompt.md file: a planning tool that helps you tell AI exactly what you want before you ask for it. The difference between a vague CSS prompt and a specific one is bigger than you'd expect. Today you'll see exactly why."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "name": "Accessibility in Design",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a before-and-after comparison of two pizza ordering websites — one confusing, one improved — and asks students to notice what changed and what could go wrong if no one fixed the first version. That \"what could go wrong\" question is the lesson's moral center: accessibility failures aren't just aesthetic problems, they're barriers that determine whether someone can participate at all. Students work as Inclusive Designers, starting with the striking statistic that nearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues, running audits on Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA, and then learning that AI reproduces these patterns by default because it learns from the web that already exists. The POUR framework gives students a professional evaluation tool they apply to real-world outputs.",
        "preparation": "- Print or make available copies of the Accessibility in Design Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nNearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues. That's not a small problem — that's most of the internet failing a significant portion of its users. Today you're going to see that up close. You'll audit three well-known websites — Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA — using a professional framework called POUR, and you'll find real problems on real sites. Then you'll connect what you found to AI: where do those problems come from, and what does it mean that AI learned from websites like these?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
      "name": "Wireframes",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a comparison: which is easier to change, a detailed polished design or a rough structural sketch? The answer sets up everything that follows. Students do two rapid sketch sprints — a home page, then an about page — for the same fictional music playlist app, comparing their Round 1 and Round 2 sketches to notice how using formal wireframe components makes layouts clearer and more structured. The lesson formally introduces abstraction alongside wireframing: when you strip out colors and details and keep only structure, that's not a limitation, it's a thinking skill. Students then wireframe their own app in Sketch Lab and give and receive peer feedback using the \"I like / I wonder / I wish\" format.",
        "student_overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nWhich is easier to change: a finished, polished design or a rough sketch made of boxes and lines? Today you'll find out why designers always start with the sketch. You'll do two quick one-minute sprints to wireframe a music playlist app — first instinctively, then using a set of standard components — and you'll probably notice your second version is clearer than your first. Then you'll apply that same thinking to your own app. What you wireframe today is what you'll hand to AI next lesson, so clarity matters."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "name": "Interactivity in Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nStudents get their first encounter with the DOM as the live, programmable representation of a webpage. They distinguish events from elements, see how JavaScript connects user actions to DOM updates, and meet accessibility through page structure — screen readers use heading hierarchy as a navigation map. The lesson lands the mental model that drives the rest of the unit: interactivity = user action triggers a JavaScript event that changes the DOM.",
        "preparation": "- Provide copies of the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide (one per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**What does it mean for a web app to respond to a user?**\n\nApps respond when you tap, swipe, and click — but how? This lesson opens the hood on the Document Object Model (DOM), the system that lets JavaScript change what's on the page. You spot the difference between actions (events) and parts of the page (elements), watch a single click change what you see, and start noticing who else might be using the page besides you."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Interactivity in Web Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "name": "Making a Button Respond",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nStudents write their first event handler. A kinesthetic role-play — Element, User, Listener, Handler — grounds the vocabulary before the JavaScript appears on screen. This is also the unit's first explicit AI literacy moment: \"AI Confidence ≠ AI Correctness\" and \"Context Blindness\" are named, with a Show / Describe / Confirm debugging protocol when AI gives students code that doesn't fit their page. Front-End Developer enters as a real role through Maya's day-in-the-life chatbot interview.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nA button isn't just a button — something is listening for your click, and something else handles what happens next. You learn the difference between an event listener and an event handler, write your first JavaScript that makes a page respond, and meet Maya, a Front-End Developer who builds this kind of thing every day. You also see your first moment of an AI giving you confident, wrong code — and learn how to catch it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
      "name": "Variables and Live Updates",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nStudents meet variables as the way apps \"remember\" state and learn the create-update-display pattern. The lesson centers QA Engineer as a role and introduces a bug-triage workflow: severity, expected vs. actual, hypothesis-first. The three Evaluate AI patterns — Is the output what you asked for? Does it work? Can you explain it? — give students shared vocabulary for critiquing AI suggestions instead of just accepting them.",
        "preparation": "- Prepare blank sheets of paper for students to use in the changing values simulation activity\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app “remember” what the user did and show it on screen?**\n\nApps remember things — your score, your cart, your scroll position — using variables. You learn how to create them, update them, and display them on the page. You also step into the shoes of a QA Engineer: the person whose job is to find bugs before users do. By the end of class, you know how to write a clean bug report and how to tell whether AI's \"fix\" actually fixes anything."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Variables and Live Updates",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "name": "Writing Reusable Functions",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nStudents learn arrow functions, named vs. anonymous syntax, return values, and abstraction — with the framing that clean, well-named code helps humans and AI work with shared code. The spec.md activity introduces literal [Specify], [Predict], and [Return] tags as a handoff from natural-language planning to AI-generated code. \"Write Rewind on your own\" is the unit's first declared Write First task — AI Tutor will not write it.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nRepeating the same lines of code over and over gets messy fast. Functions let you write a chunk of code once and reuse it everywhere. You learn to define and call arrow functions, use return values to send information back from a function, and see why naming matters for you, your teammates, and the AI tools you work with. You also write a quick plan in `spec.md` before you ask AI to generate code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "name": "Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nStudents meet flowcharts and conditional statements together. The lesson names computer vision as the AI capability that lets students hand a sketched flowchart to AI as a visual spec — connecting visual planning directly to code generation. The Warm Up plants a Domain 4 hook (\"Who decided that liking one video means showing similar ones?\"), opening a conversation that returns in later lessons.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nApps make decisions all day — show this if logged in, otherwise show that. The tool for planning those decisions is a flowchart, and the code that runs them is the `if/else` statement. You sketch your first flowchart, hand it to AI as a visual spec, and check whether the code AI writes actually follows your plan. You also start asking a bigger question: who decided the rules the apps you use follow?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
      "name": "Loops and Counted Repetition",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can a for loop help an app repeat something the right number of times?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how for loops help apps repeat an action based on user input. They begin with a no-code example where an app displays one cat emoji for each number the user enters, then connect that idea to iteration, for loops, loop variables, and start, stop, and step values. Students use a debugging checklist to test whether an AI-generated loop repeats the correct number of times, then design a counted-repetition flowchart and use AI to translate it into pseudocode.",
        "student_overview": "**How can a for loop help an app repeat something the right number of times?**\n\nToday you learn how apps use for loops to repeat an action a specific number of times. You start by thinking about an app that shows one cat emoji for each number a user enters, then learn how start, stop, and step values control a loop. You also test AI-generated loop code to check whether it repeats too many, too few, or the right number of times."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Adding NOT and Complex Conditions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
      "name": "Reactive Logic Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nThe unit capstone — five days, four milestones, one student-designed interactive app. Students plan with a wireframe and flowchart, build, test, peer-review (TAG: Tell / Ask / Give), and finalize. Reflection prompts at each milestone push students to distinguish what AI did from what they did, building toward the Claim step they'll formalize in later units. Every Phase 1 concept comes together here: event-driven design, variables, functions, and conditionals including combined conditions.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Reactive Logic Project Rubric (one for each student)\n- Print copies of the Peer Feedback with TAG handout (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can I design and build an interactive web app that responds to users in meaningful ways?**\n\nFive days. One app. Your idea, your plan, your code. You use what you've learned this unit — events, variables, functions, return values, if/else, for loops, and user-centered design — to build an interactive app that responds to the user. You plan it, build it in code, get feedback from your peers, and reflect on what worked, what didn't, and where AI helped vs. where you figured it out yourself. By the end, you have something to show — and to keep."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: Reactive Logic Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "foundations-of-ai-programming-js"
      }
    }
  ],
